00:03:53.839 --> 00:04:00.240
So before we get started, maybe we go back a little bit in history to about three years back,

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:07.759
where, yeah, I think we're just trying to get the slides in. So three years back, where our

00:04:07.759 --> 00:04:17.600
statistical computing environment was aging, right, and entering end of life. And the environment was

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:25.119
fragmented as well. So we were on SAS Grid, which had served us pretty well through the years.

00:04:25.839 --> 00:04:32.399
But as new studies kept coming in, and then the data complexity grew, and then there was a need

00:04:32.399 --> 00:04:39.920
of flexibility from various users, the platform was not able to scale. So we were faced with

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:46.480
five main issues towards us. The first one was, as I mentioned, the aging SAS infrastructure.

00:04:47.119 --> 00:04:51.200
The SAS Grid, which had served us so well through the years, was approaching end of life.

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:55.119
And it was on-prem, and because of it, it was constrained on scale,

00:04:55.839 --> 00:05:03.600
in terms of scaling on volume and compute capacity. The other problem we had was that there was a

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:11.359
great need for open source computing platform. There were people who wanted to use languages

00:05:11.359 --> 00:05:16.399
such as R, Python, but they were not able to use it because there was no centralized platform

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:21.600
to enable that capability. And the other thing was that a lot of these

00:05:22.959 --> 00:05:29.519
capabilities did exist in pockets, in the sense that there would be RStudio available on laptops,

00:05:29.519 --> 00:05:34.880
which people would use, but there was no integrated platform through which open

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:40.640
source computing could be done. The third issue, as I mentioned earlier, was that there was

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:48.160
a fragmented landscape, in the sense that data was existing in various silos, and the data was

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:54.160
spread across various storage systems. So people had to actually manually fetch the data from one

00:05:54.160 --> 00:06:00.239
store to another, and that resulted in duplication of data, as well as additional burden in bringing

00:06:00.239 --> 00:06:07.839
the data in. So that was a big issue as well. And all this led to challenges with the collaboration

00:06:07.839 --> 00:06:13.279
and interoperability. The issue was that people might code in SAS in one environment,

00:06:13.279 --> 00:06:17.440
but they would not be able to carry forward that code or collaborate with others in another

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:22.559
environment. The same thing happened for people who are coding in R or Python. So there was no

00:06:22.559 --> 00:06:27.440
good way of collaborating, and that was really because of lack of shared storage, as well as a

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:33.440
common governed environment. So all this led to the final issue, which was the biggest, which is

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:44.079
essentially the lack of governance and standards. So the lack of governance led to a lack of

00:06:45.119 --> 00:06:51.359
rule-based access controls on the data. And this was very important in a validated environment,

00:06:51.359 --> 00:06:56.559
because blinding, unblinding controls are necessary in this particular environment.

00:06:56.559 --> 00:07:02.880
And this was extremely hard to manage because of the fragmented data landscape, as well as tools

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:10.079
which were scattered all around the landscape. So we took a look at this problem, and then we

00:07:10.079 --> 00:07:16.799
felt that the easy solution is really to upgrade, in the sense that why don't we just go from one

00:07:16.799 --> 00:07:22.000
version of SAS Grid to the next, and we can address some of those issues. But we took a

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:26.959
different approach, in the sense that we took this as an opportunity to sort of reboot and then look

00:07:26.959 --> 00:07:33.359
at what we had, and then think on how we can convert this system from an analytical system

00:07:33.359 --> 00:07:37.760
to a system which can result in better decisions. So next slide, please.

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:45.519
So every journey needs to be backed by a bold vision. And our vision was to accelerate the

00:07:45.519 --> 00:07:51.440
journey from data to decision. So that's what we wanted to accelerate here. And this was founded on

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:57.600
five main pillars. The first pillar was to establish a connected scalable infrastructure.

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:02.239
So that means we have infrastructure which is based on cloud rather than on-prem which we had

00:08:02.239 --> 00:08:08.079
earlier. And then we make sure that this infrastructure is scalable, both for compute

00:08:08.079 --> 00:08:13.920
as well as storage. And we wanted AI layer to be integrated with it, not come in as an afterthought.

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:20.799
The second pillar which we wanted to build was to have a unified tool and user experience.

00:08:20.799 --> 00:08:26.880
So what it means is we wanted people to use the tool of their choice seamlessly, whether it was

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:33.359
R, Python, SAS, Scala, or any other language. And they use it in a single environment which is

00:08:33.359 --> 00:08:38.239
shared by everyone so that they can collaborate. The third one is important because this really

00:08:38.239 --> 00:08:43.760
leads us from infrastructure and tool usage to decision support. Because we wanted people to use

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:49.520
tools autonomously, but we wanted the decision support to be united. So that means everyone

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:53.919
is looking at the same data, the same governed content, and use the tools they choose.

00:08:55.119 --> 00:08:59.599
And the fourth pillar which we wanted to build was to have a single source of truth.

00:08:59.599 --> 00:09:04.960
So that means we wanted all the data, all the clinical data, to be united in one validated

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:10.159
source so that people would be making decisions off that single source itself. And there would

00:09:10.159 --> 00:09:16.159
be no manual handoffs from one point to another. And finally, what we wanted to do is support all

00:09:16.159 --> 00:09:21.280
of these pillars by overarching governance. So that means we wanted to have a GXP-validated

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:28.640
environment. And we wanted to make sure that there was rule-based access controls built in.

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:34.159
And we also have robust audit trails throughout the system end-to-end. So that way,

00:09:34.159 --> 00:09:41.039
every decision is traceable. So that was our vision to build this system. Next slide, please.

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:48.559
So we had this vision. So on what path does this vision put us in? What is the destination?

00:09:48.559 --> 00:09:52.320
How does it bring us to our end goal? So that's the question here.

00:09:53.359 --> 00:09:59.280
So the point here is we no longer wanted to stop at analysis, but we wanted to make sure

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:02.960
that the platform or the system results in decision-making. So that is our end goal.

00:10:03.599 --> 00:10:08.559
And our goal was to use these foundational pillars to work towards that goal.

00:10:08.719 --> 00:10:13.520
So if you look at this diagram left to right, then, as I mentioned earlier, we wanted to make

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:20.880
sure that the data is all centralized and sourced into one single location or a data store. And this

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:26.640
data is governed, and it is validated. So we wanted to centralize the data. And using this

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:32.320
centralized data, which is trustworthy, the analysts, scientists, as well as statisticians,

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:36.719
they could write code on the language of their choice, whether it's R, Python,

00:10:36.719 --> 00:10:42.000
or SAS. And this would be AI-enabled. And what it means is that their focus would not be data

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:47.919
wrangling or bringing in the data, but rather they would be answering the business problems.

00:10:47.919 --> 00:10:52.239
They would be looking at the business questions and what is the appropriate code to answer those

00:10:52.239 --> 00:10:56.960
business questions. That's what would be their focus. And that naturally would lead to insights

00:10:56.960 --> 00:11:01.440
in the sense that that would be an analysis performed, which would be around a decision

00:11:01.919 --> 00:11:06.880
performed, which would be around a decision question, like, say, a hypothesis. For example,

00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:11.760
is this biomarker effective or not? So that would be a question which would be sort of

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:16.479
answered through this analysis. And there could be other questions as well. So that's what is

00:11:16.479 --> 00:11:22.400
the insight. So, so far, what we have looked at is the traditional analytical platform.

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:27.119
And many of the systems really stop here, in the sense that they would write code,

00:11:27.119 --> 00:11:32.239
they would provide some insights, they would probably bring in some analytics as well.

00:11:32.239 --> 00:11:37.840
And what they would do is maybe create a nice report and then pass it on to their stakeholders.

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:44.239
So that's where analytical foundation stops. But we do not want to end here. What we wanted to do

00:11:44.239 --> 00:11:49.840
is layer this with a decision support system. And what that means is building apps or applications

00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:55.280
on top of these insight layer, so that an intelligent evaluation can be done. So what

00:11:55.280 --> 00:11:59.919
this means is we look at all these evidences, we look at all these data points, we look at all

00:11:59.919 --> 00:12:07.520
these analytics, we look at all these decision points. And based on that, we do a evaluation.

00:12:07.520 --> 00:12:13.919
And based on that evaluation, we can provide recommendations which can result in the next

00:12:13.919 --> 00:12:19.679
action. So the analysts or the statisticians or scientists can look at all these data points,

00:12:19.679 --> 00:12:24.239
and then go from one point to another, and then look at various decisions and then feed into the

00:12:24.239 --> 00:12:30.719
next decision. So that's what this application does. So it transforms the analytics into decision

00:12:30.719 --> 00:12:34.799
points. But at this point of time, these are nothing more than recommendations, because

00:12:34.799 --> 00:12:40.320
what would happen next is the most important part, which is HITL, right? And traditionally,

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:45.440
we interpret that as human in the loop. But the way I look at it is it's human in the lead,

00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:50.479
in the sense that we have scientists, statisticians looking at this recommendation,

00:12:50.479 --> 00:12:58.000
and based on that, making the decision and logging the call. So that's how we envisioned that

00:12:58.640 --> 00:13:04.000
we would lay the foundation of a decision support system, which is beyond mere analytics.

00:13:04.960 --> 00:13:09.280
If you look at the bottom of this diagram, you can see these dotted lines, which is providing

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:15.599
feedback. So this is essentially the feedback loop, which takes the various decision logs.

00:13:16.559 --> 00:13:21.760
It takes usage analytics, the observability, as well as telemetry, which is collected from the

00:13:21.760 --> 00:13:28.640
various systems, and then feeds it back. So what it essentially does is it conveys information about

00:13:28.640 --> 00:13:33.679
what calls were made, who made it, when they made it, why they made it, all that information is

00:13:33.679 --> 00:13:38.799
passed back, along with information on whether AI contributed or not. So that is the information

00:13:38.799 --> 00:13:44.799
which feeds back and based on that, the decisions are refined. So each time a decision is made,

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:50.000
it is better than the last one, because there is a feedback loop. Next slide, please.

