data sources. They come in various flavors. You have the EDC data, which in our case is

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:46.000
metadata RAM. And then you also have non-EDC data, which is essentially data coming from labs,

00:14:46.719 --> 00:14:53.840
imaging sources, IoT devices, various external vendors, as well as real-world data. So these are

00:14:53.840 --> 00:15:00.400
data which are sort of real-world evidence, claims data, for example. So all these data are ingested

00:15:00.400 --> 00:15:05.679
into a data store where we have two partitions. One is the raw partition, where the data is

00:15:06.479 --> 00:15:11.760
unmodified. It is as received, and we also have a timestamped version. So that is what is the raw

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:17.440
data. But then we also have a process which would convert that raw data into standardized clinical

00:15:17.440 --> 00:15:23.039
data model. And the purpose of this standard clinical model is to really have a common

00:15:23.039 --> 00:15:30.080
vocabulary so that downstream systems can use the data without the need to map it into a standardized

00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:37.359
structures. So this common clinical data model itself would lay the basis of standardization

00:15:37.359 --> 00:15:42.880
and would be the common source to various downstream systems. So now this is the data store.

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:47.760
And now we come to the interesting part, which is essentially our unified analytical platform,

00:15:47.760 --> 00:15:54.239
which is the statistical computing environment which we have built. So this comprises of the

00:15:54.239 --> 00:16:00.559
compute layer. The compute layer has SAS Viya, which is our upgraded version of SAS. And then

00:16:00.559 --> 00:16:06.559
we also have Posit here, which supports open source programming. It supports our Python,

00:16:06.559 --> 00:16:12.159
Scala, and other languages as well. And we also have the third component, which is high-performance

00:16:12.159 --> 00:16:17.679
computing for parallel computing loads, as well as very complex workload, which needs a lot of

00:16:17.679 --> 00:16:22.880
computation power. So we have HPC clusters supporting it. And we also have Databricks,

00:16:22.880 --> 00:16:32.320
which takes care of ML workloads, as well as big data in the sense that if we have petabytes,

00:16:32.320 --> 00:16:37.679
terabytes of data coming from real-world sources, then Databricks is the place to go where it can

00:16:38.320 --> 00:16:45.760
crunch this data. So we have all these different platforms. But the magic here is they are all

00:16:45.760 --> 00:16:50.719
integrated. And then they all talk to one another. And that is really achieved through the common

00:16:50.719 --> 00:16:56.799
shared storage. So we have a common shared storage, which all these different platforms

00:16:56.799 --> 00:17:04.640
share and use. And by means of that, the developers or the analysts who are using

00:17:04.640 --> 00:17:08.959
each of these platforms are able to collaborate and talk to each other through the common shared

00:17:08.959 --> 00:17:16.239
storage. So we also have a governance layer, which takes care of access management, the role-based

00:17:16.239 --> 00:17:21.760
access controls, as well as audit trails and administration of the whole platform itself,

00:17:21.760 --> 00:17:26.880
which sits on top of it. And it is a common governance layer. And finally, we also have

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:32.400
version control in the sense that we make sure that versioning is done for any code which goes

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:37.920
into any of these platforms, whether it's SAS Viya, or it is POSIT, or it is HCP or Databricks.

00:17:37.920 --> 00:17:43.839
We make sure that there is version control sitting on top of it. And then we also have CICD enabled.

00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:52.000
So this is essentially the unified analytical platform. And we have also built AI and

00:17:52.000 --> 00:17:57.760
agentic layer. And this sits on top of previous slide, please. So that sits on top of the data

