There's always observability and telemetry that we baked in for other components.

00:44:29.609 --> 00:44:35.609
But now we're doing that specifically for the usage of AI and AI generated code.

00:44:35.609 --> 00:44:40.609
Code accepts, code rejects, and many of those kind of considerations.

00:44:40.609 --> 00:44:44.609
And from a strategic importance standpoint, I mean, we did this not to just be cool.

00:44:44.609 --> 00:44:46.609
It is very cool.

00:44:46.609 --> 00:44:51.609
We did this because we know that if we provide an environment like this, for example,

00:44:51.609 --> 00:44:56.609
translational scientists who do biomarker research, we're going to retain that talent.

00:44:56.609 --> 00:44:57.609
We're going to attract new talent.

00:44:57.609 --> 00:45:02.609
People are going to want to be here and regenerate on doing cool science to medicine stuff, right?

00:45:02.609 --> 00:45:08.609
We truly believe that user experience really trumps a lot of things.

00:45:08.609 --> 00:45:12.609
We want to bring therapies to the market faster.

00:45:12.609 --> 00:45:19.609
As you noticed, if each scientist is spending a very large amount of time in data wrangling and things like that,

00:45:19.609 --> 00:45:23.609
that's not contributing to the bottom line.

00:45:23.609 --> 00:45:30.609
We want to stay abreast in this world of AI and how AI can improve analytics.

00:45:30.609 --> 00:45:37.609
We want to do that in a way where it's scalable, not only from a speed and that perspective,

00:45:37.609 --> 00:45:39.609
but also from a cost perspective.

00:45:39.609 --> 00:45:43.609
And we are keeping governance in mind forefront.

00:45:43.609 --> 00:45:45.609
It was always important to us.

00:45:45.609 --> 00:45:50.609
It's mandated by regulatory agencies, and we will continue to hold that.

00:45:50.609 --> 00:45:54.609
And the final thing that I want to conclude with, by the numbers here,

00:45:54.609 --> 00:46:03.609
we have a whole lot of people on this system in its entirety, 400 plus users,

00:46:03.609 --> 00:46:08.609
whether that's developers or people who essentially consume reports of the Viya ecosystem.

00:46:08.609 --> 00:46:14.609
I believe that there is twice as much of that on the Posit system at this point.

00:46:14.609 --> 00:46:17.609
Once again, a mix of developers and consumers.

00:46:18.609 --> 00:46:24.609
We have just about, I heard that that number grew recently of how much data was migrated to the system.

00:46:24.609 --> 00:46:27.609
I think we probably have more than 100 terabytes at this point,

00:46:27.609 --> 00:46:32.609
more than 1,000 apps that was developed on Posit over the course of the last three years in our journey.

00:46:32.609 --> 00:46:37.609
And many publications, many files transferred during the migrations,

00:46:37.609 --> 00:46:44.609
and 100 molecules, essentially, worth of data that are in there.

00:46:44.609 --> 00:46:47.609
So that's the big picture.

00:46:47.609 --> 00:46:56.609
We went from having a first principles design on an analytical platform,

00:46:56.609 --> 00:47:02.609
evolved with the times, with AI, and with everything else that is going on,

00:47:02.609 --> 00:47:07.609
into what we truly see now is as a decision platform,

00:47:07.609 --> 00:47:13.609
interactivity, and the ability to iterate, and the ability to have an AI enablement.

00:47:13.609 --> 00:47:19.609
And like I said before, one decision itself made very fast,

00:47:19.609 --> 00:47:23.609
but then that decision leading to another, and then that cycle endlessly continuing.

00:47:23.609 --> 00:47:28.609
I think that is how we see our business users benefiting.

00:47:28.609 --> 00:47:33.609
And that is why we truly feel that the era of analytical platforms is dead.

00:47:33.609 --> 00:47:38.609
And we are open arms welcoming the era of decision platforms.

00:47:44.609 --> 00:47:49.609
Thank you very much, Dilip, Sriram, and Anand.

00:47:49.609 --> 00:47:55.609
Hi, everyone. I'm Samuel. I'm a solution advisor at Posit Life Sciences team.

00:47:55.609 --> 00:47:59.609
So we have a bunch of questions. We'll see if we can cover all of them,

00:47:59.609 --> 00:48:03.609
and I will help you guys go through. So I'll read this out.

00:48:03.609 --> 00:48:10.609
First question from Stefan, what specific metrics does your AI telemetry capture?

00:48:10.609 --> 00:48:15.609
For example, acceptance, edit, and rejection rates of AI suggestions,

00:48:15.609 --> 00:48:22.609
share of code AI generated per program, and validation findings for AI versus human written code?

00:48:22.609 --> 00:48:28.609
Yeah, great question. I think I started answering this in the chat.

00:48:28.609 --> 00:48:37.609
So just as a continuation of that, our philosophy of how we deploy AI to do a code generation

00:48:37.609 --> 00:48:48.609
is essentially AI as an orchestrator to utilize governed, validated modules of code that has always existed, right?

00:48:48.609 --> 00:48:52.609
An organization our size with many people doing analysis for many years,

00:48:52.609 --> 00:48:58.609
we already have a very robust library of code that is validated and rigorously tested, etc.

00:48:58.609 --> 00:49:03.609
Essentially, we are employing AI as an orchestrator of doing that,

00:49:03.609 --> 00:49:08.609
and also at the points where it's not able to sort of flag that out.

00:49:08.609 --> 00:49:15.609
At this point, all of that sort of interactivity is within just the developers, right?

00:49:15.609 --> 00:49:19.609
What we ultimately want to do is bring about this, and this is exactly,

00:49:19.609 --> 00:49:25.609
I was so pleased to see Posit Commons as a product or solution go exactly in this space,

00:49:25.609 --> 00:49:30.609
which is whether it is a developer or if it is a ultimate end business user,

00:49:30.609 --> 00:49:34.609
you ask a question, you want it to generate code or produce an output,

00:49:34.609 --> 00:49:40.609
how much of that underlying background work was done by AI using validated code, robust code,

00:49:40.609 --> 00:49:44.609
the things that you controlled, versus how much of it was generated by it on the fly,

00:49:44.609 --> 00:49:48.609
and how confident is it that it generated the right code, right?

00:49:48.609 --> 00:49:55.609
That's sort of a multi-layered sort of metric is certainly where we're going to.

00:49:55.609 --> 00:50:01.609
And in terms of telemetry, yes, we want to capture the number of times it,

00:50:01.609 --> 00:50:03.609
for example, like the false positive, false negatives,

00:50:03.609 --> 00:50:06.609
like number of times it claims that it's using a code from the code base,

00:50:06.609 --> 00:50:11.609
but it's not, the number of times it is using, for example,

00:50:11.609 --> 00:50:17.609
it's creating a new code base while something else existed in the repository.

00:50:17.609 --> 00:50:22.609
So we want to kind of see all these various combinations of how it performs.

00:50:22.609 --> 00:50:27.609
And with that level of telemetry, with the accepts and rejects from the actual developers

00:50:27.609 --> 00:50:32.609
or the business users, that is the next phase of how we're going about.

00:50:32.609 --> 00:50:35.609
So great question. That's exactly what we're thinking about.

00:50:35.609 --> 00:50:38.609
We haven't done it yet, but we know how important that is.

00:50:38.609 --> 00:50:40.609
And I think there is a follow-up to that.

00:50:40.609 --> 00:50:43.609
And I know many folks are asking this question.

00:50:43.609 --> 00:50:46.609
Is AI-assisted code being used for submission?

00:50:46.609 --> 00:50:49.609
And if not, what needs to be done to make it happen?

00:50:49.609 --> 00:50:52.609
I love this topic. I love this topic. I'll take this one.

00:50:52.609 --> 00:50:55.609
So here's our philosophy, right?

00:50:55.609 --> 00:51:00.609
And this is, if you look at what the FDA is saying, what the EMA is saying,

00:51:00.609 --> 00:51:06.609
like what the general guidance is around AI or usage of AI in submissions,

00:51:06.609 --> 00:51:11.609
or regulatory submissions, especially with analysis and outputs created using analysis,

00:51:11.609 --> 00:51:18.609
everybody would want you to define the use cases of how you're using the AI

00:51:18.609 --> 00:51:19.609
and what you're planning to achieve.

00:51:19.609 --> 00:51:21.609
They do want you to test that process.

00:51:21.609 --> 00:51:27.609
They do want you to go through all the rigorous process of how you do a computer system validation today.

00:51:27.609 --> 00:51:34.609
The idea behind, are you using a code generated by an AI in submissions?

00:51:34.609 --> 00:51:44.609
I mean, we have been having code assistance in a way, right, throughout, even before AI,

00:51:44.609 --> 00:51:50.609
whether that's modules, macros, whether that's just go on and find a bunch of code online,

00:51:50.609 --> 00:51:54.609
which does a particular thing and sort of take that and integrate it into your code base

00:51:54.609 --> 00:51:56.609
and sort of run it, test it, et cetera.

00:51:56.609 --> 00:52:02.609
It always went back to the developer as your accountability

00:52:02.609 --> 00:52:05.609
to make sure that you have architected that code well,

00:52:05.609 --> 00:52:10.609
that you've optimized it for accuracy and runtime and efficiencies and so on and so forth.

00:52:10.609 --> 00:52:14.609
I don't think any of that is different now that we're in the age of AI.

00:52:14.609 --> 00:52:19.609
Yes, a lot of those individual steps can be fine-tuned by AI.

00:52:19.609 --> 00:52:23.609
Maybe AI becomes more confident and the confidence core sort of drives you,

00:52:23.609 --> 00:52:27.609
looking at a lesser amount of things than previously.

00:52:27.609 --> 00:52:34.609
But I feel that that is really not much different than what it was before.

00:52:34.609 --> 00:52:39.609
So whether or not to use a piece of code generated by AI in a submission,

00:52:39.609 --> 00:52:43.609
well, that code, whether it was generated by a human or a computer,

00:52:43.609 --> 00:52:48.609
needs to have gone through certain specific stage gates.

00:52:48.609 --> 00:52:56.609
The stage gates are now, in an old world, SOPs, which did not have anything about AI.

00:52:56.609 --> 00:53:03.609
In the new world, we're anticipating that there are a few more stage gates to be added

00:53:03.609 --> 00:53:07.609
or a few more documentations to be produced through the existing stage gates

00:53:07.609 --> 00:53:14.609
to highlight the fact that certain things were reviewed and approved and so on and so forth.

00:53:14.609 --> 00:53:19.609
But we fully expect that AI-generated code will be used in submissions

00:53:19.609 --> 00:53:25.609
and it will follow the same rigor that we've always followed for the last 70 plus years.

00:53:25.609 --> 00:53:32.609
Yeah, I think as long as you can trace back who is responsible for generating the code, I think.

00:53:32.609 --> 00:53:35.609
And I always think it is like a self-driving car, right?

00:53:35.609 --> 00:53:39.609
Like who will take the responsibility when you're using a self-driving car?

00:53:39.609 --> 00:53:42.609
So next question from Devam.

00:53:42.609 --> 00:53:51.609
Are you separating GXP deliverables from non-GXP exploratory deliverables in this platform?

00:53:51.609 --> 00:53:54.609
Yeah, I can actually take that question.

00:53:54.609 --> 00:53:56.609
Let's see. I think we're good.

00:53:56.609 --> 00:53:57.609
So I'll take that question.

00:53:57.609 --> 00:54:03.609
So the question is whether we are separating the GXP deliverables from non-GXP deliverables.

00:54:03.609 --> 00:54:04.609
Again, a good question.

00:54:04.609 --> 00:54:08.609
And that's something we touched upon in our presentation as well.

00:54:08.609 --> 00:54:11.609
So we wanted to keep the migration journey separate.

00:54:11.609 --> 00:54:16.609
Like we wanted to do the non-GXP first, followed by GXP, right?

00:54:16.609 --> 00:54:22.609
And the rigor also differs because you need many more deliverables

00:54:22.609 --> 00:54:27.609
and then many more artifacts supporting your GXP compared to non-GXP.

00:54:27.609 --> 00:54:33.609
And also we wanted to make sure that when you do a non-GXP, the lessons learned can feed into GXP, right?

00:54:33.609 --> 00:54:35.609
So that that journey is much smoother.

00:54:35.609 --> 00:54:37.609
So that is the approach we took.

00:54:37.609 --> 00:54:41.609
And the short answer to the question, are we keeping the deliverables separate?

00:54:41.609 --> 00:54:42.609
The answer is yes.

00:54:42.609 --> 00:54:49.609
It has to be kept separate because, first of all, the environment itself is separate for both of them.

00:54:49.609 --> 00:54:56.609
And then the deliverables which need to be tracked and then audited and then reviewed are also different.

00:54:56.609 --> 00:55:02.609
So for that matter, I think we sort of keep the deliverables separate between these two environments.

00:55:02.609 --> 00:55:06.609
I don't know, Anand, if you have anything to add here.

